  "text": "None of the three solutions can be confirmed, and Solution 1 is definitely wrong.\n\n**Solution 1 (22) is wrong.** Since $K_4 \\subseteq K_5 - e$, we get $R(K_5, K_5-e) \\ge R(K_5, K_4) = 25$, so 22 is impossible. The value 22 is $R(K_5-e, K_5-e)$, not the pair the problem asks about.\n\n**Solution 2 (30) is unsupported.** The lower bound argument is not valid as written: the step \"$\\frac{3(R(K_4,K_4)-1)}{2}+1 = 26.5$\" is not a standard result and the bound it yields is not clearly derived. The claimed construction on $K_{29}$ and the upper-bound proof for $n=30$ are asserted without verifiable detail, and the \"pattern\" table is not evidence. I cannot verify the claimed upper bound.\n\n**Solution 3 (34) is also unsupported.** Its lower bound of 25 and its citation $R(K_5-e,K_5-e)=22$ are reasonable. But the claimed exact value rests on vague references to \"edge-counting\" and \"computer-assisted verification\" with no specifics. I also do not recall an established exact value for $R(K_5, K_5-e)$ in the standard small-Ramsey-number surveys. If the value is open, then no solution's claim of an exact value can be correct.\n\nI could not check the literature, so I cannot rule out that one of 30 or 34 matches a published result I don't recall. On the evidence I can assess, no solution is shown to be correct.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
